{"id":819,"date":"2020-11-06T11:59:37","date_gmt":"2020-11-06T19:59:37","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=819"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:41:33","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:41:33","slug":"height","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/height\/","title":{"rendered":"4C: Height"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2216762e88a\" name=\"6a2216762e88a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>How does this work?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Quick review: the barometer in your CoDrone Mini measures the surrounding air pressure. Generally, air pressure is greatest at sea level and then decreases as the altitude, or height, increases.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">With your CoDrone Mini, you will be using the barometer to check sea level and then the altitude from the floor. This can be done once or multiple times in one program.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2216762e9b4\" name=\"6a2216762e9b4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>get_height()<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The function that you will be using to measure the height of your drone from the floor is \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">get_height()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After checking the sea level with the barometer and then the altitude from the floor, it will return the distance between those two points in centimeters.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try out this example program to see what it looks like in action!\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.hover(3)\r\n\r\nheight = drone.get_height()\r\n\r\nprint(height)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2216762ea3c\" name=\"6a2216762ea3c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Takeoff height<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Get the height of your drone before takeoff, while it\u2019s in the air, and after it lands!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After importing the library, creating the drone object, and pairing with CoDrone Mini, create a variable that will get the drone\u2019s height on the floor.<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">We named ours \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">height_1<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Write a takeoff command after it.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, create another variable that will get the drone\u2019s height while it is in the air (ours is \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">height_2<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">) and include a land command after that. Create one more variable that will get the drone\u2019s height after landing.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Finally, write three print commands that will display the drone\u2019s heights at each part of its flight.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This is what your program should look like:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nheight_1 = drone.get_height()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\nheight_2 = drone.get_height()\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\nheight_3 = drone.get_height()\r\n\r\n\r\nprint(\"Before taking off: \", height_1)\r\n\r\nprint(\"After taking off: \", height_2)\r\n\r\nprint(\"Landed: \", height_3)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Once you\u2019ve seen this, play around with your program! What happens if you move the print statements around or add more flight movements?<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2216762eb86\" name=\"6a2216762eb86\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Measuring height in a loop<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">get_height()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function can be used in a loop to measure one height after the other. In this program, you\u2019re going to measure the altitude of your drone as it keeps throttling up.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To start, import the CoDrone Mini and time libraries, create the drone object, and then pair to CoDrone Mini. Next, create a variable called<\/span> \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">throttle<\/code>\u00a0<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">and set it to 20, which is its power percentage. Then reset your drone\u2019s barometer.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This next part is optional! If you see that your drone is drifting in a certain direction instead of flying straight up, you can include the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">set_trim()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function to fix the roll and pitch. If you decide to do this, include a sleep command so that your takeoff isn\u2019t skipped.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Include a print command that will print the drone\u2019s initial height, which should be the floor. Next, create a list called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">height_data<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. This will store all of the altitudes that your drone reaches.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Set up a for loop that repeats three times. Your drone should takeoff and throttle up for 1.5 seconds at the percentage stored in the throttle variable, which starts out at 20. The \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">get_height()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function should then get the altitude and add that value to the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">height_data()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">list. The drone should then land and then sleep for 5 seconds, which will give you time to reposition it if needed. Next, a value of 10 should be added to <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">throttle<\/code>.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After the loop, include a command to print the data that was stored in the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">height_data()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">list. Does the data look like what you expected?<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your program should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nthrottle = 20\r\n\r\ndrone.reset_sensor()\r\n\r\ndrone.set_trim(25,15) # set your own trim values\r\n\r\ntime.sleep(1) # to avoid skipping takeoff()\r\n\r\nprint(\"Initial: \", drone.get_height())\r\n\r\nheight_data = []\r\n\r\nfor i in range(3):\r\n\r\n    drone.takeoff()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_throttle(throttle)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.move(1.5)\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 height_data.append(drone.get_height())\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.land()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(5) # gives time to reposition your drone on the ground\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 throttle = throttle + 10\r\n\r\nprint(height_data)\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2216762ed3c\" name=\"6a2216762ed3c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge: Fact Checker<\/b><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Have CoDrone Mini fly to three different places in the room you\u2019re in, like to the top of a closet, the top of a bookcase, and the top of a chair. Include print commands so you know how many centimeters the altitude is at each location. Next, get a meter stick or measuring tape and see if the height of each place matches up with what was printed out!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 6 How does this work? [vc_column_text]Quick review: the barometer in your CoDrone Mini measures the surrounding air pressure. Generally, air pressure is greatest at sea level and then&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/height\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1729,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_